-- SIMD Benchmark — Vec4 Dot Product (10 million iterations)
--
-- WHY CLEAR FAILS HERE:
--
--   CLEAR has no SIMD vector types and no way to express data-parallel
--   operations. The transpiler generates plain Zig structs with individual
--   field accesses. The dot4 function emits:
--
--     return ((((a.x * b.x) + (a.y * b.y)) + (a.z * b.z)) + (a.w * b.w));
--
--   That is 4 scalar fmul + 3 scalar fadd = 7 serial FP operations per call.
--
--   C uses explicit AVX2 intrinsics:
--     - _mm256_set_pd:  load all 4 doubles into one 256-bit register
--     - _mm256_mul_pd:  multiply all 4 pairs in a SINGLE hardware instruction
--     - _mm_hadd_pd:    horizontal reduction
--
--   AVX2 processes 4 doubles in parallel, so the multiply phase is ~4x fewer
--   instructions. FMA (_mm256_fmadd_pd on wider loops) fuses multiply+add.
--
-- NOTE: Zig ReleaseFast uses LLVM -O3 which may auto-vectorize the reduction
--   loop in cheatMain. Results depend on whether LLVM can prove the
--   cross-iteration independence. The C baseline uses explicit intrinsics to
--   guarantee SIMD usage regardless of auto-vectorization decisions.
--
-- MEASURED RESULTS (x86-64 Linux, AVX2+FMA, ReleaseFast / -O3 -mavx2 -mfma):
--
--   VARIANT                | TIME      | vs C
--   C (explicit AVX2)      | ~44 ms    | 1.0× (baseline)
--   CLEAR (scalar Zig)     | ~62 ms    | ~1.4×
--
-- Binary inspection confirms:
--   C:     vmulpd ymm  (256-bit, 4 doubles per instruction)
--   CLEAR: vmulsd xmm  (scalar,  1 double per instruction)
--
-- WHY THE GAP IS ONLY ~1.4× NOT ~4×:
--   Two overheads mask the full SIMD benefit in CLEAR:
--   1. rt.saveLoopMark() / rt.restoreLoopMark() called on EVERY loop
--      iteration (10M function calls) — inhibits loop vectorization.
--   2. `_ = &a` in generated Zig forces Vec4 struct to stack memory
--      (prevents register allocation) — inhibits auto-vectorization.
--   The horizontal reduction in the C AVX2 path (hadd) also adds
--   latency, slightly reducing the 4× theoretical maximum.
--
-- CONCLUSION: CLEAR does not support SIMD. Even when LLVM cannot
--   auto-vectorize due to runtime call barriers, the scalar code is
--   only ~1.4× slower than explicit AVX2 on this workload — but the
--   potential ceiling is 4× (or 8× with float32) for pure compute loops.

STRUCT Vec4 {
    x: Float64,
    y: Float64,
    z: Float64,
    w: Float64
}

-- Scalar dot product: 4 fmul + 3 fadd.
-- No SIMD equivalent exists in CLEAR today.
FN dot4(a: Vec4, b: Vec4) RETURNS Float64 @reentrant ->
    RETURN a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y + a.z*b.z + a.w*b.w;
END

FN cheatMain() RETURNS Void ->
    MUTABLE sum: Float64 = 0.0;
    MUTABLE i = 0_i64;
    WHILE i < 100000000 DO
        a = Vec4{ x: i+0.0, y: i+1.0, z: i+2.0, w: i+3.0 };
        b = Vec4{ x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0, w: 4.0 };
        sum = sum + dot4(a, b);
        i = i + 1_i64;
    END
    ASSERT sum > 0.0, "sum must be positive";
    RETURN;
END
